'''tiresol†''' (tēr′sol), ''n.'' [< OF. *''tiresol'', < ''tirer'', draw, + ''sol'', sun: see ''tire''<sup>2</sup> and ''sol''<sup>1</sup>.] A sun-umbrella; a sunshade.

{{smaller block|Next to whom cometh the King with a ''Tiresol'' ouer his head, to keepe off the Sunne.
{{right|''Purchas'', Pilgrimage, p. 538.}}}}

'''tiresome''' (tīr′sum), ''a.'' [< ''tire''<sup>1</sup> + ''-some''.] 1. Tending to tire; exhausting the strength; fatiguing: as, a ''tiresome'' journey.

{{smaller block|Being of a weak constitution, in an employment precarious and ''tiresome'', . . . this new weight of party malice had struck you down.
{{right|''Swift'', To Dr. Sheridan, Sept. 11, 1725.}}}}

2. Exhausting the patience or attention; wearisome; tedious; prosy.

{{smaller block|It would be ''tiresome'' to detail all the troubles of the Copts under the tyranny of the Moos'lims.
{{right|''E. W. Lane'', Modern Egyptians, II. 337.}}
The bees keep their ''tiresome'' whine round the resinous firs on the hill.
{{right|''Browning'', Up at a Villa.}}
His generosity to his troops of ''tiresome'' cousins has been, at all events, without graciousness.
{{right|''The Academy'', May 11, 1889, p. 330.}}}}

{{smaller block|='''Syn.''' 1 and 2. ''Tedious'', ''Irksome'', etc. See ''wearisome''.—2. Dull, humdrum.}}

'''tiresomely''' (tīr′sum-li), ''adv.'' In a tiresome manner; wearisomely.

'''tiresomeness''' (tīr′sum-nes), ''n.'' The state or quality of being tiresome; wearisomeness; tediousness.

{{smaller block|I should grow old with the ''Tiresomeness'' of living so long in the same Place, tho' it were Rome itself.
{{right|''N. Bailey'', tr. of Colloquies of Erasmus, I. 345.}}}}

'''tire-valiant†''' (tīr′val″yant), ''n.'' A head-dress for women.

