'''tiring-woman†''' (tīr′ing-wu̇m″an), ''n.'' A tire-woman; a female dresser, as in a theater.

{{smaller block|Elizabeth [Pepys] was particular in the choice of a ''tiring-woman.''
{{right|''The Atlantic,'' LXVI. 750.}}}}

'''tirite''' (tī′rīt), ''n.'' A reed-like West Indian plant, ''Ischnosiphon Arouma,'' of the ''Zingiberaceæ.''

'''tirl<sup>1</sup>''' (tėrl), ''v.'' [A dial. var. of ''twirl'' or of ''thirl''<sup>1</sup>. Cf. ''tirl''<sup>2</sup>.] '''I.''' ''intrans.'' '''1.''' To quiver; vibrate; thrill; hence, to change or veer about, as the wind. ''Jamieson.''—'''2.''' To produce a rattling or whirring; make a clatter, as by shaking or twirling something.—{{smaller|'''To tirl at''' or '''on the pin,''' to shake the latch of a door by means of a projecting pin of the thumb-piece, and thus make a rattling noise as a signal to those inside that one wishes to enter. Also ''to tirl the pin.''}}

{{smaller block|Lang stood she at her true love's door,
And lang ''tirl'd at the pin.''
{{right|''Fair Annie of Lochroyan'' (Child's Ballads, II. 100).}}
Whan they cam to her father's yett [gate],
She ''tirled on the pin.''
{{right|''Earl Richard'' (Child's Ballads, III. 401).}}}}

'''II.''' ''trans.'' '''1.''' To twirl; whirl or twist.

{{smaller block|O how they bend their backs and fingers ''tirle'' [in playing an instrument]. ''Muse's Threnodie,'' p. 133. (''Jamieson.'')}}

'''2.''' To strip or pluck off quickly.

{{smaller block|And off his coat thay ''tirlit'' be the croun,
And on him kest ane syde clarkly goun.
{{right|''Priest's Peblis,'' S. P. R., i. 30. (''Jamieson.'')}}
When the wind blaws loud and ''tirls'' our strae.
{{right|''Remains of Nithsdale Song,'' p. 33.}}}}

'''3.''' To strip of something; uncover; unroof; divest, as of covering or raiment.

